  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increased humidity and warmer temperatures \ncontinue tomorrow.\n\nWarmer temperatures with increased humidity continue into\ntomorrow as an anomalous moisture plume and higher 925 mb \ntemperatures advect into southern New England via SW flow. \nLooking ahead into tonight, the increased moisture and winds \ndropping off in the interior may favor the development of some \npatchy fog, especially in the CT Valley. With 925 mb \ntemperatures reaching around 16-19C in the afternoon Thursday, \nhigh temperatures in the low 80s are a possibility, especially \nin the river valleys. These higher low level temperatures remain\nover the region overnight as well with high pressure affixed \njust to our south continuing SW flow; lows tonight will likely \nonly drop into 60s as opposed to the areas of 50s and 40s we've \nseen the past few nights. PWATs up to 2.0\" and dewpoints in the \nupper 60s will make it feel more humid, too. Despite the \nincreased humidity, the region should remain dry for much of the\nday tomorrow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers possible Thursday evening \ninto Friday morning. Dry during the day Friday.\n\nA weak shortwave trough progresses eastward behind the plume of \nmoisture, which will aid in the development of scattered showers\nacross southern New England. As previously mentioned, latest \nguidance has shown PWATs reaching ans even exceeding 2.0\" \nThursday afternoon/evening. However, we are not anticipating \nwidespread downpours as guidance continues to track the stronger\nforcing across northern parts of New England. Global models and\na number of the CAMs (with the exception of the NAM) are in \nagreement that higher rainfall totals should generally remain to\nour north, and ensemble probabilities also favor this outcome. \nTotals across southern New England should primarily range \nbetween 0.1\" to 0.3\" based on the more northern track of the \nwave. The latest CAMs have still shown some elevated instability\nThursday night, so a low chance of some isolated downpours \ncannot be completely ruled out. Totals could also increase \nslightly if the wave were to end up tracking a little further \nsouth, but the general consensus is that the greatest forcing \nwill remain to our north and we could generally expect a few \ntenths of an inch.\n\nFollowing this system, drier air pushes back in with NW flow,\nwhich will aid in clearing skies through the day and bringing \ntemperatures back to a more seasonable range. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Rain chances return some time late Saturday\nnight into Monday morning. There remains uncertainty in the \ndetails such as coverage, timing, amounts. \n\nA high pressure should move across northern New England\nSaturday, then over the North Atlantic Sunday. This should \nclear the path for a low pressure to move from the lower Great \nLakes south of New England late some time late Saturday night \ninto Monday. This will likely be our next period of rainfall. \nHowever, the timing is not yet finalized, especially with a high\npressure over the ocean. Would not be surprised if a slower \ntiming develops.\n\nThere is also a question about how much rainfall we can expect \nacross our region. High pressure over eastern Canada may nudge \nfar enough south to pus the ultimate storm track far enough \nsouth where we could wind up dry Tuesday into Wednesday. \nAssuming this occurs, total rainfall amounts could be limited to\na general 1-2 inches by Monday afternoon. Otherwise, with a \nnearly stationary front close to the south coast of New England \ncould trigger some showers right into mid week.",
